# Product Documentation

Product documentation for {{PROJECT_NAME}} lives in a three-tier hierarchy: platform-level specifications that apply across all surfaces, app-level specifications tied to a specific surface's interaction model, and package-level code artifacts (types, schemas, domain models) that carry shared concepts into code.

## Hierarchy

### Platform level (`/docs/product/`)

Product governance and cross-surface specifications. Artifacts here describe what the product does, why, and architectural decisions that apply regardless of surface.

- `constitution.md` — approved, versioned product constitution required before the first PRD
- `vision.md` — approved, versioned north-star product vision required before the first PRD
- `features/{feature}/PRD.md` — product requirements (what and why)
- `features/{feature}/TRD.md` — technical requirements (how, architecturally)
- `features/{feature}/stories/` — user stories decomposing the PRD's requirements
- `features/{feature}/scenarios/` — platform-level behavioral contracts (`.feature` files in Gherkin)

### App level (`/apps/{surface}/docs/`)

Surface-specific specifications. Artifacts here describe behaviors and interactions that are bound to a specific surface's interaction model.

- `features/{feature}/scenarios/` — surface-specific `.feature` files (e.g., {{SURFACE_SCENARIO_EXAMPLE}})

Step definitions (automation code that implements both platform and surface scenarios) live in each app's test directory, not in `docs/`.

### Package level (`/packages/*/`)

Shared types, schemas, hooks, and domain models. Documentation at this level is code-level: READMEs, JSDoc/TSDoc, and inline documentation. Packages carry the "one domain model, many surfaces" principle into code.

## Artifact flow

Each artifact type references the one above it:

```
Constitution
  ↓
Vision (approved, versioned north star)
  ↓
PRD (checks constitution and vision alignment)
  ↓
TRD (references PRD requirements)
  ↓
User Stories (decompose PRD requirements into user-facing narratives)
  ↓
Platform Scenarios (verify stories' acceptance criteria as behavioral contracts)
  ↓
Surface Scenarios (extend with interaction-specific behaviors)
  ↓
Step Definitions (automate scenarios as executable tests)
```

## Foundation gate

Before the first feature PRD, `docs/product/constitution.md` and `docs/product/vision.md` must exist, be approved, and carry explicit version metadata. If either foundation document is missing, unapproved, or unversioned, complete the Foundation Gate before starting feature requirements.

When `grill-with-docs` is used, product-domain context may live in `docs/product/CONTEXT.md`; if so, root `CONTEXT-MAP.md` should point to it. `CONTEXT.md` is for glossary and domain language, not requirements. ADRs default to `docs/adr/` unless this repository already uses another ADR convention.

## Boundary rule

A specification belongs at **platform level** if it holds true regardless of whether the user is on {{SURFACE_LIST_PROSE}}.

> "{{PLATFORM_SCENARIO_EXAMPLE}}"

A specification belongs at **app level** if its language references a surface-specific interaction model.

> "{{SURFACE_SCENARIO_EXAMPLE_FULL}}"

When in doubt, start at platform level. Move to app level only when the Given/When/Then language cannot be written without referencing a specific interaction model.

## Directory structure

```
docs/product/
├── README.md                 ← this file
├── AGENTS.md                 ← guidance for AI coding agents
├── constitution.md           ← approved, versioned product constitution
├── vision.md                 ← approved, versioned product vision
├── CONTEXT.md                ← optional product-domain context for grill-with-docs
└── features/
    └── {feature}/
        ├── PRD.md            ← product requirements
        ├── TRD.md            ← technical requirements
        ├── stories/          ← user stories (.md)
        └── scenarios/        ← platform-level .feature files

apps/{surface}/
├── docs/
│   └── features/
│       └── {feature}/
│           └── scenarios/    ← surface-specific .feature files
└── tests/ or __tests__/
    └── features/
        └── {feature}/
            └── steps/        ← step definitions (all scenarios)
```
